<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation by default. Test projects opt out by
    setting <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    in their csproj. (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because
    the SDK locks in its decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every ProjectReference builds in the configuration its consumer was asked
    for. halheinrich/Math#47.

    Without this, a Release build of this member links the *Debug*
    BigRationalLibrary, and every "0 warnings in Release" and "passes in
    Release" ever recorded here was measured against a Debug producer. This
    repository has exactly one reference that can be affected: the one in
    RationalApproximation.csproj that escapes the repository altogether, to
    ..\..\BigRationalLibrary. RationalApproximation.slnx lists only this
    member's own two projects, and a solution assigns a configuration only to
    the projects it contains.

    Measured here before and after this property, both times a solution build
    at `-c Release -t:Rebuild`: before, the BigRationalLibrary.dll copied into
    this repository's bin/Release differed from the producer's own Release
    output; after, the two are identical.

    That is stated as the comparison rather than as a pair of hashes on
    purpose — halheinrich/Math#49. The SDK embeds the producer's HEAD sha in
    AssemblyInformationalVersion, so a producer hash identifies a *commit*
    rather than a source state and is false as soon as the producer commits
    again. The check is: build the producer in Release, build this member in
    Release with -t:Rebuild, and compare the two BigRationalLibrary.dll files
    by hashes computed in the same run. tools/check-release-linkage.sh in the
    umbrella is what does that, and it is what verified this change rather
    than the session's own report.

    Why the metadata that looks like the obvious fix is not the fix. Setting
    SetConfiguration on the ProjectReference is applied at evaluation and then
    overwritten at build, measured in four rows on the issue by the
    ContinuedFractions session. In Microsoft.Common.CurrentVersion.targets on
    SDK 10.0.400, line 1584 defaults ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform
    to true whenever BuildingSolutionFile or BuildingInsideVisualStudio is
    set, and line 1609 hands it to the AssignProjectConfiguration task, whose
    job is then to rewrite SetConfiguration on every reference absent from the
    solution map, unsetting the parent's configuration so the referenced
    project falls back to its own default.

    Line 1584's condition names BuildingInsideVisualStudio as well, so this
    was never confined to `dotnet build <solution>`: Release builds inside
    Visual Studio 2026 linked the Debug producer too. CI never saw it from
    either direction — .github/workflows/build-and-test.yml builds the
    solution with no -c and is therefore Debug-only, so the one configuration
    this defect lives in is the one configuration CI does not build. Nothing
    about the workflow changes here.

    Flagged rather than quietly relied on, on three counts. It overrides an
    MSBuild default instead of working with it. The property is not part of
    the documented public surface; it lives in the SDK's targets, which is
    where it was read, so an SDK upgrade may change it with no error and a
    silently wrong binary as the symptom. And it propagates Platform as well
    as Configuration, which is harmless only while every member is
    AnyCPU-only. The default exists so a solution configuration is not forced
    onto a project that may not define it; every member here defines exactly
    Debug and Release with no platform variation, so the condition it guards
    against does not arise. If either premise changes, this is the decision to
    revisit.

    Preferred over listing BigRationalLibrary in this member's solution, which
    also works and uses only supported solution semantics: membership puts a
    foreign project inside this member's solution, scales with dependency
    depth as the graph grows, and when incomplete it fails silently, restoring
    exactly this bug with exactly this symptom.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>false</ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately — and here that overrides a stated decision
    rather than waking a gate that never fired. This was gated on
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild, and unlike the other members carrying this
    file, this repo has a workflow that sets it: build-and-test.yml passes
    -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true on restore, build and test, at lines
    130, 136 and 145. Measured on SDK 10.0.400 with that workflow's own
    restore command, the gate is live — a changed PackageReference version
    fails there with NU1004 today. The previous comment's "only enabled in CI
    so local `dotnet add package` keeps working" described a real tradeoff,
    not an oversight, and removing the condition gives that convenience up on
    purpose.

    What is bought. The guarantee held only where a switch set in another
    directory happened to be passed, so the file could not state its own
    promise; and locally it did not hold at all. A local restore *updates* a
    lock rather than enforcing it, which is how a committed lock elsewhere in
    this project sat at preview1 against a preview2 producer for weeks,
    through four arcs, with every local build passing. Unconditional gives
    the working tree the guarantee CI already had.

    What is paid is exactly the convenience the old rationale named:
    `dotnet add package`, and any deliberate version edit, now fails with
    NU1004 instead of quietly updating the lock. Recover with
    `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`, which is what the CLI's
    force-evaluate switch sets, and commit the regenerated lock. One command,
    once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole purpose is that it
    does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here as a property
    because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.)

    What it does not buy, measured here rather than assumed: locked mode
    compares the lock against PackageReference versions, not against the
    version a ProjectReference contributes. A ProjectReference version bump
    leaves the lock stale and restore passes in locked mode regardless, so
    that kind of change still needs a force-evaluated restore to reach the
    lock — nothing below will catch it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
